# Build context exclusions.
#
# There was no .dockerignore here, so every image build shipped the whole
# repository root — including .git — to the daemon, and `COPY . .` in the main
# Dockerfile then laid the host's node_modules over the one `npm ci` had just
# built one layer earlier. That last part is not hypothetical: this repo tracked
# a node_modules SYMLINK until 3449fbb, so the context genuinely carried one.
#
# BEFORE ADDING ANYTHING HERE, note that a .dockerignore applies to every
# Dockerfile built from this context, and the three here do not need the same
# files:
#
#   Dockerfile           COPY . .          → needs src/, scripts/, tsconfig.json
#                                            (src/catalog/generated too — the
#                                            build's copy-generated-assets step
#                                            reads it)
#   Dockerfile.migrate   COPY migrations   → needs migrations/
#   Dockerfile.backfill  explicit COPYs    → needs tsconfig.json, src/, scripts/
#
# So migrations/, src/, scripts/ and tsconfig.json must never be excluded, even
# though the first Dockerfile alone would not miss some of them. Everything
# listed below is consumed by none of the three.

# Never needed to build, and by far the largest thing in the context.
.git
.github

# Installed inside the image by `npm ci`. Excluding it is what stops a host
# install — or a stray symlink — from overwriting the image's own copy.
node_modules

# Regenerated by `npm run build` inside the image; a host build's output here
# would be stale at best and a different Node/TS version's at worst.
dist
coverage

# Local tooling and editor noise.
.husky
.DS_Store
*.log

# Never let a local environment file reach an image layer.
.env
.env.*
